{
  "id": "cyrillic",
  "entries": [
    {
      "id": "at-a-glance",
      "topic": "overview",
      "title": "Russian at a glance",
      "body": [
        "Russian is an East Slavic language written in Cyrillic. Nouns, adjectives and pronouns change for six cases, and there are three genders. There are no articles, and no word for is in the present tense.",
        "Because endings show who does what, word order is flexible and is used for emphasis: the new or important information usually comes last.",
        "Verbs come in pairs of imperfective and perfective aspect, which matters more than tense."
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "t": "Я студент.",
          "r": "Ya student.",
          "e": "I am a student."
        },
        {
          "t": "Маша читает книгу.",
          "r": "Masha chitayet knigu.",
          "e": "Masha is reading a book."
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "Slavic",
        "cases",
        "aspect"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "false-friends",
      "topic": "script",
      "title": "Letters that look Latin but aren't",
      "body": "Several Cyrillic letters look like Latin ones with a different sound. These cause the most mistakes at the start.",
      "table": {
        "head": [
          "Letter",
