    {
      "id": "vowel-pairs",
      "topic": "script",
      "title": "Vowel pairs and the two signs",
      "body": [
        "Each vowel has a hard and a soft partner. The soft vowels make the consonant before them soft (palatalized), and at the start of a word they add a y sound.",
        "Two letters have no sound of their own: {ь} (the soft sign) softens the consonant before it, and {ъ} (the hard sign) keeps a consonant hard before a soft vowel."
      ],
      "table": {
        "head": [
          "Hard",
          "Soft",
          "Example"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "а · a",
            "я · ya",
            "мясо · myaso · meat"
          ],
          [
            "э · e",
            "е · ye",
            "лето · leto · summer"
          ],
          [
            "ы · y",
            "и · i",
            "быть · byt' · to be; бить · bit' · to hit"
          ],
          [
            "о · o",
            "ё · yo",
            "ёж · yozh · hedgehog"
          ],
          [
            "у · u",
            "ю · yu",
            "юг · yug · south"
          ]
        ]
      },
      "examples": [
        {
          "t": "мат · мат{ь}",
          "r": "mat · mat'",
          "e": "checkmate · mother"
        },
        {
          "t": "под{ъ}езд",
          "r": "pod'yezd",
          "e": "entrance (of a building)"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "script",
        "vowels",
        "soft sign"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "stress-reduction",
      "topic": "sounds",
      "title": "Stress and vowel reduction",
      "body": [
        "Stress can fall on any syllable and has to be learned with each word. Only the stressed vowel is said fully: an unstressed о sounds like a, and unstressed е and я sound close to i. Textbooks mark stress with an accent; normal text does not.",
        "{ё} is always stressed, but it is often printed as е."
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "t": "молоко́",
          "r": "malakó",
          "e": "milk: two unstressed о said as a"
        },
        {
          "t": "хорошо́",
          "r": "kharashó",
          "e": "good, well"
        },
        {
          "t": "за́мок · замо́к",
          "r": "zámak · zamók",
          "e": "castle · lock"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "pronunciation",
        "stress"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "devoicing",
      "topic": "sounds",
      "title": "Final devoicing",
      "body": "At the end of a word, and before a voiceless consonant, voiced consonants lose their voice: б → p, в → f, г → k, д → t, ж → sh, з → s.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "t": "хлеб",
          "r": "khlep",
          "e": "bread"
        },
        {
          "t": "го́род",
          "r": "górat",
          "e": "city"
        },
        {
          "t": "в{т}о́рник",
          "r": "ftórnik",
          "e": "Tuesday (в before т)"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "pronunciation"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "gender",
      "topic": "grammar",
      "title": "Gender from the ending",
      "body": "You can tell a noun's gender from its ending almost every time. Nouns in -ь can be either and need to be learned; men's names and words like папа are masculine even though they end in -а.",
      "table": {
        "head": [
          "Gender",
          "Ending",
          "Examples"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "masculine",
            "consonant, -й",
            "стол · table; музей · museum"
          ],
          [
            "feminine",
            "-а, -я",
            "книга · book; неделя · week"
          ],
          [
            "neuter",
            "-о, -е",
            "окно · window; море · sea"
          ],
          [
            "either",
            "-ь",
            "день · day (m); ночь · night (f)"
          ]
        ]
      },
      "tags": [
        "nouns",
        "gender"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cases",
      "topic": "grammar",
      "title": "The six cases",
      "body": "Each case answers its own question word. Learn them with the questions, as Russian schoolchildren do.",
      "table": {
        "head": [
          "Case",
          "Question",
          "Main uses",
          "книга"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Nominative",
            "кто? что?",
            "subject",
            "книга"
          ],
          [
            "Genitive",
            "кого? чего?",
            "of, none of, after numbers, у (by, at)",
            "книги"
          ],
          [
            "Dative",
            "кому? чему?",
            "to, for; indirect object; age; мне нравится",
            "книге"
          ],
          [
            "Accusative",
            "кого? что?",
            "direct object; motion into (в, на)",
            "книгу"
          ],
          [
            "Instrumental",
            "кем? чем?",
            "with, by means of; after с (with)",
            "книгой"
          ],
          [
            "Prepositional",
            "о ком? о чём?",
            "about (о); location (в, на)",
            "о книге"
          ]
        ]
      },
      "tags": [
        "cases",
        "nouns"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "to-be-have",
      "topic": "grammar",
      "title": "To be and to have",
      "body": [
        "There is no present tense of to be: just put the two nouns side by side. In writing, a dash may stand in its place.",
        "To have is said as at me there is: у + genitive + есть."
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "t": "Это мой дом.",
          "r": "Eto moy dom.",
          "e": "This is my house."
        },
        {
          "t": "Москва — столица России.",
          "r": "Moskva — stolitsa Rossii.",
          "e": "Moscow is the capital of Russia."
        },
        {
          "t": "{У меня есть} кот.",
          "r": "U menya yest' kot.",
          "e": "I have a cat."
        },
        {
          "t": "{У меня нет} кота.",
          "r": "U menya net kota.",
          "e": "I don't have a cat. (нет + genitive)"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "to be",
        "to have"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "pronouns",
      "topic": "grammar",
      "title": "Pronouns and ты / вы",
      "body": "{ты} is you to one friend, family member, child or animal. {вы} is you to several people, and the polite you to anyone you'd call by name and patronymic. When in doubt, use вы.",
      "table": {
        "head": [
          "",
          "Singular",
          "Plural"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "1st",
            "я · I",
            "мы · we"
          ],
          [
            "2nd",
            "ты · you",
            "вы · you (plural, polite)"
          ],
          [
            "3rd",
            "он · he; она · she; оно · it",
            "они · they"
          ]
        ]
      },
      "tags": [
        "pronouns",
        "politeness"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "present-tense",
      "topic": "verbs",
      "title": "Present tense: two conjugations",
      "body": "Most verbs follow one of two patterns. First conjugation verbs have -е- in their endings; second conjugation verbs, mostly those ending in -ить, have -и-.",
      "table": {
        "head": [
          "Person",
          "читать (read)",
          "говорить (speak)"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "я",
            "читаю",
            "говорю"
          ],
          [
            "ты",
            "читаешь",
            "говоришь"
          ],
          [
            "он · она",
            "читает",
            "говорит"
          ],
          [
            "мы",
            "читаем",
            "говорим"
          ],
          [
            "вы",
            "читаете",
            "говорите"
          ],
          [
            "они",
            "читают",
            "говорят"
          ]
        ]
      },
      "tags": [
        "verbs",
        "conjugation"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "past-tense",
      "topic": "verbs",
      "title": "Past tense agrees in gender",
      "body": "The past tense is the easiest: drop -ть and add -л, then a gender and number ending. It agrees with the subject's gender, not with person.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "t": "Он чита{л}.",
          "r": "On chital.",
          "e": "He was reading. / He read."
        },
        {
          "t": "Она чита{ла}.",
          "r": "Ona chitala.",
          "e": "She was reading."
        },
        {
          "t": "Оно чита{ло}.",
          "r": "Ono chitalo.",
          "e": "It was reading."
        },
        {
          "t": "Они чита{ли}.",
          "r": "Oni chitali.",
          "e": "They were reading."
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "verbs",
        "past"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "aspect",
      "topic": "verbs",
      "title": "Aspect: imperfective and perfective",
      "body": "Most verbs come in pairs. The {imperfective} describes a process, a repeated action or the action in general. The {perfective} describes one completed action with a result. Perfective verbs have no present tense: their present-looking forms mean the future.",
      "table": {
        "head": [
          "Imperfective",
          "Perfective",
          "Meaning"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "делать",
            "сделать",
            "do, make"
          ],
          [
            "писать",
            "написать",
            "write"
          ],
          [
            "читать",
            "прочитать",
            "read"
          ],
          [
            "говорить",
            "сказать",
            "say"
          ],
          [
            "покупать",
            "купить",
            "buy"
          ]
        ]
      },
      "examples": [
        {
          "t": "Я {читал} книгу.",
          "r": "Ya chital knigu.",
          "e": "I was reading a book. / I read a book (at some point)."
        },
        {
          "t": "Я {прочитал} книгу.",
          "r": "Ya prochital knigu.",
          "e": "I read the book (to the end)."
        },
        {
          "t": "Я {прочитаю} книгу.",
          "r": "Ya prochitayu knigu.",
          "e": "I will read the book."
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "verbs",
        "aspect"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "motion",
      "topic": "verbs",
      "title": "Verbs of motion",
      "body": "Going has separate verbs for on foot and by vehicle, and each comes in two forms: one for a single trip in one direction, one for round trips, habits and general movement.",
      "table": {
        "head": [
          "",
          "One way, now",
          "Habitual, there and back"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "on foot",
            "идти",
            "ходить"
          ],
          [
            "by vehicle",
            "ехать",
            "ездить"
          ]
        ]
      },
      "examples": [
        {
          "t": "Я {иду} в школу.",
          "r": "Ya idu v shkolu.",
          "e": "I'm on my way to school."
        },
        {
          "t": "Я {хожу} в школу каждый день.",
          "r": "Ya khozhu v shkolu kazhdyy den'.",
          "e": "I go to school every day."
        },
        {
          "t": "Мы {едем} в Москву.",
          "r": "My yedem v Moskvu.",
          "e": "We're travelling to Moscow."
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "verbs",
        "motion"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "numbers",
      "topic": "numbers",
      "title": "Numbers and the noun after them",
      "body": "один, два, три, четыре, пять, шесть, семь, восемь, девять, десять. The noun after a number changes case: after 1 it stays nominative singular, after 2–4 it takes genitive singular, after 5–20 genitive plural. The last digit decides above 20: 21 год, 22 года, 25 лет.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "t": "один год",
          "r": "odin god",
          "e": "one year"
        },
        {
          "t": "два год{а}",
          "r": "dva goda",
          "e": "two years"
        },
        {
          "t": "пять {лет}",
          "r": "pyat' let",
          "e": "five years"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "numbers",
        "cases"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrases",
      "topic": "phrases",
      "title": "Everyday phrases",
      "examples": [
        {
          "t": "Здравствуйте",
          "r": "Zdravstvuyte",
          "e": "Hello. (polite; the first в is silent)"
        },
        {
          "t": "Привет",
          "r": "Privet",
          "e": "Hi. (to friends)"
        },
        {
          "t": "Спасибо",
          "r": "Spasibo",
          "e": "Thank you."
        },
        {
          "t": "Пожалуйста",
          "r": "Pozhaluysta",
          "e": "Please. You're welcome."
        },
        {
          "t": "Извините",
          "r": "Izvinite",
          "e": "Excuse me. Sorry."
        },
        {
          "t": "Как дела?",
          "r": "Kak dela?",
          "e": "How are things?"
        },
        {
          "t": "Я не понимаю",
          "r": "Ya ne ponimayu",
          "e": "I don't understand."
        },
        {
          "t": "До свидания",
          "r": "Do svidaniya",
          "e": "Goodbye."
        },
        {
          "t": "Пока",
          "r": "Poka",
          "e": "Bye. (to friends)"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "greetings"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "adjectives",
      "topic": "grammar",
      "title": "Adjective endings",
      "body": "Adjectives agree with the noun in gender, number and case, and come before it. The dictionary form is masculine nominative. Soft-stem adjectives like синий (dark blue) use the soft vowels.",
      "table": {
        "head": [
          "",
          "Masculine",
          "Feminine",
          "Neuter",
          "Plural"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "новый · new",
            "новый дом",
            "новая книга",
            "новое окно",
            "новые дома"
          ],
          [
            "синий · blue",
            "синий дом",
            "синяя книга",
            "синее окно",
            "синие дома"
          ],
          [
            "большой · big",
            "большой дом",
            "большая книга",
            "большое окно",
            "большие дома"
          ]
        ]
      },
      "tags": [
        "adjectives",
        "agreement"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "where-whither",
      "topic": "grammar",
      "title": "в / на: where vs where to",
      "body": "в and на take the {prepositional} for location (where?) and the {accusative} for direction (where to?). Some places use на rather than в: work, the post office, concerts, the street.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "t": "Я {в школе}.",
          "r": "Ya v shkole.",
          "e": "I'm at school. (где? prepositional)"
        },
        {
          "t": "Я иду {в школу}.",
          "r": "Ya idu v shkolu.",
          "e": "I'm going to school. (куда? accusative)"
        },
        {
          "t": "Она {на работе}.",
          "r": "Ona na rabote.",
          "e": "She's at work."
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "prepositions",
        "cases"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "like",
      "topic": "grammar",
      "title": "I like: мне нравится",
      "body": "Russian says it pleases me. The liked thing is the subject, and the person who likes it is in the dative. The verb agrees with the thing.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "t": "{Мне} нравится музыка.",
          "r": "Mne nravitsya muzyka.",
          "e": "I like music."
        },
        {
          "t": "{Ему} нравятся кошки.",
          "r": "Yemu nravyatsya koshki.",
          "e": "He likes cats. (plural verb for plural cats)"
        },
        {
          "t": "{Мне} нравилось там.",
          "r": "Mne nravilos' tam.",
          "e": "I liked it there."
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "dative",
        "verbs"
      ]
    }
  ]
}
